DECLARE `Kind of reference in the Schedule` IS ONE OF
    `a reference to a section`
    `a reference to a contract`
    `a reference to anything else`

DECLARE `What a reference in the Schedule refers to` IS ONE OF
    `a section of this Act`
    `a contract of sale of goods`

§§ `Paragraph 1 -- preliminary`

-- paragraph 1(1), gathered by hand (E1 option B) from the windows of paragraphs 2 to 13.
-- src:1659 | 1.—(1) This Schedule modifies this Act as it applies to contracts of sale of goods
-- src:1660 | made on certain dates.
@ref sch para 1(1) (src:1659-1660)
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sch para 1(1) the Schedule modifies this Act as it applies to the contract` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `helper (Schedule): the date falls within the window` `the date the contract was made` (Date 1 1 1894) (Date 19 5 1985)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the paragraph says the Schedule modifies the Act "as it applies to contracts of sale of goods made on certain dates"; the dates are those in paragraphs 2 to 13 (the latest window ends before 19 May 1985) and paragraph 1(3) excludes a contract made before 1 January 1894. -- ruled 2026-11-05 (a): a period the text fixes in its own words; outside it the answer is no
            JUST FALSE

-- paragraph 1(2).  A rule of reading for the Schedule's own words.
-- src:1662 | (2) In this Schedule, references to sections are to those of this Act and references
-- src:1663 | to contracts are to contracts of sale of goods.
@ref sch para 1(2) (src:1662-1663)
GIVEN `the kind of reference` IS A `Kind of reference in the Schedule`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What a reference in the Schedule refers to`
DECIDE `sch para 1(2) what a reference in the Schedule refers to` IS
    CONSIDER `the kind of reference`
    WHEN `a reference to a section` THEN JUST `a section of this Act`
    WHEN `a reference to a contract` THEN JUST `a contract of sale of goods`
    WHEN `a reference to anything else` THEN
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph speaks only of "references to sections" and "references to contracts".
        NOTHING

-- paragraph 1(3).
-- src:1665 | (3) Nothing in this Schedule affects a contract made before 1 January 1894.
@ref sch para 1(3) (src:1665)
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sch para 1(3) the Schedule affects the contract` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the date the contract was made` AT LEAST (Date 1 1 1894))
        THEN
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Nothing in this Schedule affects a contract made before 1 January 1894."
            JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph speaks only of "a contract made before 1 January 1894".
            NOTHING

§§ `Paragraph 2 -- section 11: condition treated as warranty`

-- paragraph 2: s 11(3) with the insertion "or where the contract is for specific goods, the property in which has passed to the buyer,".
-- src:1669 | 2. In relation to a contract made before 22 April 1967, in section 11(3) after “or
-- src:1670 | part of them,” insert “or where the contract is for specific goods, the property in
-- src:1671 | which has passed to the buyer,”.
@ref sch para 2 (src:1669-1671)
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
      `the contract of sale is severable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract is for specific goods, the property in which has passed to the buyer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an express or implied term of the contract allows rejection` IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 35A preserves the buyer's right to reject` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sch para 2 s 11(3) as modified: the breach of a condition can only be treated as a breach of warranty` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the date the contract was made` AT LEAST (Date 22 4 1967))
        THEN `s 11(3) the breach of a condition can only be treated as a breach of warranty` `the contract of sale is severable` (`the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them` OR `the contract is for specific goods, the property in which has passed to the buyer`) `an express or implied term of the contract allows rejection` `section 35A preserves the buyer's right to reject`
        OTHERWISE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): paragraph 2 applies "In relation to a contract made before 22 April 1967"; the unmodified s 11(3) answers a later contract. -- ruled 2026-11-05 (a): a period the text fixes in its own words; outside it the answer is no
            JUST FALSE

§§ `Paragraph 3 -- section 12 as it stood for a contract made before 18 May 1973`

-- paragraph 3, limb (a): the implied condition that the seller has a right to sell.
-- src:1675 | 3. In relation to a contract made before 18 May 1973, substitute the following for
-- src:1676 | section 12:
-- src:1678 | . “Implied terms about title, etc.
-- src:1680 | 12. In a contract of sale, unless the circumstances of the contract are
-- src:1681 | such as to show a different intention, there is —
-- src:1683 | (a) an implied condition on the part of the seller that in the
-- src:1684 | case of a sale he has a right to sell the goods, and in the case
-- src:1685 | of an agreement to sell he will have such a right at the time
-- src:1686 | when the property is to pass;
@ref sch para 3 s 12(a) (src:1675-1686)
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
      `the circumstances of the contract are such as to show a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sch para 3 s 12(a) there is an implied condition that the seller has a right to sell the goods` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date the contract was made` AT LEAST (Date 18 5 1973)
        THEN
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): paragraph 3 applies "In relation to a contract made before 18 May 1973". -- ruled 2026-11-05 (a): a period the text fixes in its own words; outside it the answer is no
            JUST FALSE
        IF `the circumstances of the contract are such as to show a different intention`
        THEN
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the circumstances of the contract are such as to show a different intention, there is (a) an implied condition"
            -- MARGINAL (a): "unless" is read as the text's no when the circumstances show a different intention.
            JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:1675 | 3. In relation to a contract made before 18 May 1973, substitute the following for
-- src:1676 | section 12:
-- src:1678 | . “Implied terms about title, etc.
-- src:1680 | 12. In a contract of sale, unless the circumstances of the contract are
-- src:1681 | such as to show a different intention, there is —
-- src:1683 | (a) an implied condition on the part of the seller that in the
-- src:1684 | case of a sale he has a right to sell the goods, and in the case
-- src:1685 | of an agreement to sell he will have such a right at the time
-- src:1686 | when the property is to pass;
@ref sch para 3 s 12(a) breach (src:1675-1686)
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
      `the circumstances of the contract are such as to show a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract is a sale` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller has a right to sell the goods when the contract is made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller will have such a right at the time when the property is to pass` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sch para 3 s 12(a) the implied condition as to the right to sell is broken` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date the contract was made` AT LEAST (Date 18 5 1973)
        THEN
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the condition is implied in a contract "made before 18 May 1973" -- ruled 2026-11-05 (a): a period the text fixes in its own words; outside it the answer is no
            JUST FALSE
        IF `the circumstances of the contract are such as to show a different intention`
        THEN
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "unless the circumstances of the contract are such as to show a different intention" -- the exception takes the case out and the text says nothing further.
            NOTHING
        IF `the contract is a sale`
        THEN JUST (NOT `the seller has a right to sell the goods when the contract is made`)
        OTHERWISE JUST (NOT `the seller will have such a right at the time when the property is to pass`)

-- paragraph 3, limb (b): the implied warranty of quiet possession.
